                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, can you explain to me what HbA1c is?\n\nProfessor: Sure, HbA1c is a form of hemoglobin that is used to measure the average blood sugar levels over a period of 2-3 months.\n\nStudent: Okay, so what is the best method to measure HbA1c?\n\nProfessor: The best method to measure HbA1c is ion exchange chromatography.\n\nStudent: Can you explain how ion exchange chromatography works?\n\nProfessor: Sure, ion exchange chromatography separates molecules based on their charge. In this case, HbA1c has a negative charge, so it will bind to a positively charged resin. The resin is then washed to remove any other molecules that may have bound to it, and the HbA1c is eluted and measured.\n\nStudent: How does this method compare to the other options listed?\n\nProfessor: Well, isoelectric focusing separates molecules based on their isoelectric point, which is the pH at which they have no net charge. Affinity chromatography separates molecules based on their specific binding properties. Electrophoresis separates molecules based on their size and charge. While these methods can also be used to measure HbA1c, ion exchange chromatography is the most accurate and widely used method.",
