                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain why Giemsa stained smear cannot detect Coxiella burnetti?\n\nProfessor: Sure. Giemsa stain is a type of differential stain that is commonly used to detect blood parasites and bacteria. However, Coxiella burnetti is an intracellular bacterium that cannot be detected by Giemsa stain.\n\nStudent: What about Baonella? Why can't it be detected by Giemsa stain?\n\nProfessor: Baonella is actually a misspelling of the bacterium, Burkholderia. Similar to Coxiella burnetti, Burkholderia is also an intracellular bacterium that cannot be detected by Giemsa stain.\n\nStudent: How about Toxoplasmosis? Can it be detected by Giemsa stain?\n\nProfessor: Yes, Toxoplasmosis is a parasitic infection that can be detected by Giemsa stain. The stain can detect the presence of the parasite in blood smears or tissue samples.\n\nStudent: And what about E. Chaffnessis? Can it be detected by Giemsa stain?\n\nProfessor: I'm sorry, but there is no such thing as E. Chaffnessis. I think you may have meant E. chaffeensis, which is a bacterium that causes human monocytic ehrlichiosis. Similar to Coxiella burnetti and Burkholderia, E. chaffeensis is also an intracellular bacterium that cannot be detected by Giemsa stain.",
