                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Metformin is a medication commonly used to treat type 2 diabetes. It works by decreasing glucose production in the liver and increasing insulin sensitivity in the body. However, one of the potential side effects of metformin is lactic acidosis, which is a buildup of lactic acid in the bloodstream that can be severe and even life-threatening.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is lactic acidosis?\n2. How does metformin cause lactic acidosis?\n3. What are the risk factors for lactic acidosis?\n\nAnswer:\n1. Lactic acidosis is a condition where there is an excess buildup of lactic acid in the bloodstream. This can occur when the body produces too much lactic acid or when the body is unable to clear lactic acid efficiently.\n2. Metformin can cause lactic acidosis by inhibiting the liver's ability to clear lactic acid from the bloodstream. This can lead to a buildup of lactic acid in the body, which can be dangerous.\n3. The risk factors for lactic acidosis include advanced age, liver dysfunction, and renal failure. However, smoking is not a known risk factor for lactic acidosis. Therefore, the answer is B) Smoking.",
