                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Phase 1 clinical trials are the first stage of testing a new drug or treatment in humans. These trials are usually conducted on a small group of healthy volunteers to evaluate the safety, tolerability, and pharmacokinetics of the drug.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the definition of safety in the context of clinical trials?\n2. Why is safety the major aim of phase 1 clinical trials?\n3. What is the definition of efficacy in the context of clinical trials?\n4. What is the definition of dose in the context of clinical trials?\n5. What is the definition of pharmacokinetics in the context of clinical trials?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Safety in the context of clinical trials refers to the absence of harmful effects of the drug or treatment on the participants.\n2. Safety is the major aim of phase 1 clinical trials because it is important to ensure that the drug or treatment is safe for human use before proceeding to larger trials.\n3. Efficacy in the context of clinical trials refers to the ability of the drug or treatment to produce the desired therapeutic effect.\n4. Dose in the context of clinical trials refers to the amount of the drug or treatment given to the participants.\n5. Pharmacokinetics in the context of clinical trials refers to the study of how the drug is absorbed, distributed, metabolized, and eliminated by the body. This information is important for determining the appropriate dose and dosing schedule for the drug.",
