                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, can you explain what is meant by phase 1 clinical trials?\n\nProfessor: Sure, phase 1 clinical trials are the first stage of testing a new drug or treatment in humans. The main goal of phase 1 trials is to determine the safety of the drug and the appropriate dosage.\n\nStudent: Okay, so what is the major aim of phase 1 clinical trials?\n\nProfessor: The major aim of phase 1 clinical trials is to ensure the safety of the drug. So, the answer is A) Safety.\n\nStudent: How do they determine the safety of the drug in phase 1 clinical trials?\n\nProfessor: In phase 1 clinical trials, a small group of healthy volunteers are given the drug and closely monitored for any adverse effects. The dosage is gradually increased until the maximum tolerated dose is reached. This helps to determine the safety of the drug and the appropriate dosage.\n\nStudent: What about efficacy? Is that not important in phase 1 clinical trials?\n\nProfessor: Efficacy is important, but it is not the main focus of phase 1 clinical trials. The primary goal is to determine the safety of the drug. Efficacy is usually evaluated in later phases of clinical trials.\n\nStudent: And what about dose and pharmacokinetics?\n\nProfessor: Dose and pharmacokinetics are also important in phase 1 clinical trials. The appropriate dosage needs to be determined, and pharmacokinetics helps to understand how the drug is absorbed, distributed, metabolized, and eliminated by the body. However, the major aim of phase 1 clinical trials is still safety.",
