                        "cot": "\n\nThe question is asking which route of drug administration does not bypass hepatic first pass metabolism. \n\nHepatic first pass metabolism refers to the process where drugs are metabolized by the liver before they enter the systemic circulation. This can significantly reduce the bioavailability of the drug, meaning that less of the drug reaches the target site in the body. \n\nA) Oral administration involves swallowing the drug, which then passes through the gastrointestinal tract and liver before entering the systemic circulation. This route of administration bypasses hepatic first pass metabolism to some extent, as some of the drug may be absorbed directly into the bloodstream from the stomach or small intestine. \n\nB) Intravenous administration involves injecting the drug directly into a vein, bypassing the gastrointestinal tract and liver entirely. This route of administration completely bypasses hepatic first pass metabolism. \n\nC) Sublingual administration involves placing the drug under the tongue, where it is absorbed directly into the bloodstream through the sublingual mucosa. This route of administration also bypasses hepatic first pass metabolism. \n\nD) Intradermal administration involves injecting the drug into the dermis, or the layer of skin just below the epidermis. This route of administration does not bypass hepatic first pass metabolism, as the drug must still enter the systemic circulation through the bloodstream. \n\nTherefore, the answer is D) Intradermal.",
