                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: \n- Retrograde amnesia is a type of memory loss where a person is unable to recall events that occurred before the injury or trauma.\n- A fall and vomiting near an alcohol shop suggest that the person may have been under the influence of alcohol.\n- Diffuse axonal injury and concussion are types of traumatic brain injury that can result from a fall or blow to the head.\n- Cerebral venous thrombosis is a rare condition where a blood clot forms in the veins that drain blood from the brain.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is diffuse axonal injury?\n2. What is concussion?\n3. What are the symptoms of drunkenness?\n4. What is cerebral venous thrombosis?\n5. Which of these conditions is most likely to cause retrograde amnesia in a person who has fallen and vomited near an alcohol shop?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Diffuse axonal injury is a type of traumatic brain injury where the brain's axons (nerve fibers) are sheared or torn due to rotational forces. It can result in widespread damage to the brain and can cause coma or persistent vegetative state.\n2. Concussion is a type of traumatic brain injury where the brain is jolted or shaken inside the skull. It can cause a range of symptoms, including headache, dizziness, confusion, and memory loss.\n3. Symptoms of drunkenness can include slurred speech, impaired coordination, impaired judgment, memory loss, and vomiting.\n4. Cerebral venous thrombosis is a rare condition where a blood clot forms in the veins that drain blood from the brain. It can cause a range of symptoms, including headache, seizures, and changes in vision or consciousness.\n5. Based on the given information, the most likely cause of the person's retrograde amnesia is either drunkenness or concussion. Diffuse axonal injury and cerebral venous thrombosis are less likely given the person's awake state and lack of other symptoms. However, without further information or diagnostic tests, it is difficult to determine the exact cause.",
