                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the key information in the scenario.\n- 40 years old male\n- History of fall\n- One episode of vomiting near an alcohol shop\n- Brought to casualty\n- Awake with open eyes\n- Retrograde amnesia\n\nStep 2: Consider each option and eliminate those that are unlikely.\n- Drunkenness: While alcohol may have played a role in the fall and vomiting, it is unlikely to cause retrograde amnesia and other symptoms.\n- Concussion: This is a possibility, as a fall can cause a concussion. However, the presence of retrograde amnesia suggests a more severe injury.\n- Cerebral venous thrombosis: This is unlikely, as it is a rare condition that typically presents with different symptoms such as headache, seizures, and focal neurological deficits.\n\nStep 3: Determine the most likely cause based on the remaining option.\n- Diffuse axonal injury: This is the most likely cause, as it is a common injury in cases of head trauma and can cause retrograde amnesia, as well as other symptoms such as confusion, dizziness, and loss of consciousness.\n\nAnswer: A) Diffuse axonal injury",
                        "answers": [
