                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe symptoms of weakness, fatigue, and hypoglycemia suggest that the patient is not able to maintain normal blood glucose levels. This could be due to a deficiency in an enzyme involved in glucose metabolism. \n\nRaw eggs contain avidin, a protein that binds to biotin, a vitamin necessary for the function of several enzymes involved in glucose metabolism. Therefore, the doctor prescribed vitamin tablets, which likely contained biotin, to address the deficiency.\n\nThe enzyme deficiency causing hypoglycemia in this patient could be one involved in gluconeogenesis or glycogenolysis, the processes by which the body produces glucose from non-carbohydrate sources or breaks down glycogen to release glucose, respectively. \n\nOut of the options given, Phosphoenol pyruvate carboxykinase (PEPCK) and Pyruvate carboxylase are enzymes involved in gluconeogenesis, while Glycogen phosphorylase and Glucose 6 phosphatase are involved in glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis, respectively.\n\nSince the patient consumed raw eggs, which contain avidin that binds to biotin, it is likely that the enzyme deficiency is due to a lack of biotin-dependent enzymes involved in gluconeogenesis. Therefore, the correct answer is either A) Phosphoenol pyruvate carboxykinase or B) Pyruvate carboxylase. \n\nWithout further information or diagnostic tests, it is not possible to determine which of these two enzymes is deficient in the patient.",
