                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step-by-step procedure:\n1. Identify the symptoms presented by the patient: weakness, fatigue, and hypoglycemia.\n2. Determine the cause of hypoglycemia in the patient.\n3. Identify the enzyme deficiency responsible for the hypoglycemia.\n\nGoing through each step:\n1. The symptoms presented by the patient are weakness, fatigue, and hypoglycemia.\n2. Hypoglycemia can be caused by a deficiency in enzymes involved in gluconeogenesis or glycogenolysis, which are processes that produce glucose in the body. \n3. The enzyme deficiency responsible for the hypoglycemia in this patient is likely to be one involved in gluconeogenesis or glycogenolysis. \n\nOption A, Phosphoenol pyruvate carboxykinase, is an enzyme involved in gluconeogenesis. A deficiency in this enzyme can lead to hypoglycemia. \n\nOption B, Pyruvate carboxylase, is also involved in gluconeogenesis. A deficiency in this enzyme can lead to hypoglycemia. \n\nOption C, Glycogen phosphorylase, is involved in glycogenolysis. A deficiency in this enzyme can lead to glycogen storage disease, but not necessarily hypoglycemia. \n\nOption D, Glucose 6 phosphatase, is involved in both gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis. A deficiency in this enzyme can lead to hypoglycemia. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D) Glucose 6 phosphatase.",
