                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\n1. The Krebs cycle is a metabolic pathway that occurs in the mitochondria of cells and is responsible for the production of energy in the form of ATP.\n\n2. Acetyl-CoA is a molecule that enters the Krebs cycle and is formed from the breakdown of carbohydrates, fats, and proteins.\n\n3. Pyruvate is a molecule that can be converted into Acetyl-CoA and enter the Krebs cycle.\n\n4. To determine which amino acid does not enter the Krebs cycle by forming Acetyl-CoA pyruvate, we need to look at the metabolic pathways of each amino acid.\n\n5. Glycine is an amino acid that can be converted into serine, which can then be converted into pyruvate. Therefore, glycine can enter the Krebs cycle by forming Acetyl-CoA pyruvate.\n\n6. Tyrosine is an amino acid that can be converted into fumarate, which enters the Krebs cycle at a later stage. Therefore, tyrosine does not enter the Krebs cycle by forming Acetyl-CoA pyruvate.\n\n7. Hydroxyproline is an amino acid that is derived from proline and can be converted into pyruvate. Therefore, hydroxyproline can enter the Krebs cycle by forming Acetyl-CoA pyruvate.\n\n8. Alanine is an amino acid that can be converted into pyruvate. Therefore, alanine can enter the Krebs cycle by forming Acetyl-CoA pyruvate.\n\n9. Therefore, the correct answer is B) Tyrosine, as it does not enter the Krebs cycle by forming Acetyl-CoA pyruvate.",
