                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Understanding Golgi Tendon Organs\nGolgi tendon organs are sensory receptors located in the tendons of muscles. They are responsible for detecting changes in muscle tension and transmitting this information to the central nervous system.\n\nStep 2: Eliminating Options\nOption A, static muscle length, is not detected by Golgi tendon organs. This is because they are located in the tendons and not in the muscle fibers themselves. Therefore, option A can be eliminated.\n\nOption B, dynamic muscle length, is also not detected by Golgi tendon organs. This is because they are not sensitive to changes in muscle length, but rather changes in muscle tension. Therefore, option B can also be eliminated.\n\nStep 3: Identifying the Correct Option\nOption C, muscle tension, is the correct answer. Golgi tendon organs are sensitive to changes in muscle tension, which is the force exerted by the muscle on the tendon. When the muscle contracts, the tension in the tendon increases, which activates the Golgi tendon organs. This information is then transmitted to the central nervous system, which can use it to regulate muscle activity.\n\nOption D, muscle action, is not a specific enough term to be the correct answer. While Golgi tendon organs do detect muscle activity, this is a broad term that could refer to many different aspects of muscle function. Therefore, option D can be eliminated.\n\nConclusion: Golgi tendon organs detect muscle tension, which is the force exerted by the muscle on the tendon. This information is transmitted to the central nervous system, which can use it to regulate muscle activity.",
