            {
                "id": "f3d65283-cf60-4fd3-a23a-804ca33bc8e9",
                "ref_id": "",
                "question": "If the interstitial hydrostatic pressure is 2 mm Hg, interstitial oncotic pressure is 7 mm Hg and capillary hydrostatic pressure is 25 mm Hg. What should be the capillary oncotic pressure to allow a net filtration pressure of 3 mm Hg?",
                "type": "multiplechoice",
                "choices": [
                    "20",
                    "21",
                    "23",
                    "27"
                ],
                "context": "",
                "cot": [
                    "Ans: D. 27Formula - GFR = Kf l(PGC- PT) - (7r GC-)I* Kf- Glomerular ultrafiltration coefficient. - Product of glomerular capillary wall hydraulic conductivity (i.e. its permeability) & effective filtration surface area.* PGC - Mean hydrostatic pressure in glomerular capillaries = 25* PT - Mean hydrostatic pressure in tubule (Bowman's space) = 2* GC - Oncotic pressure of plasma in glomerular capillaries* T - Interstitial colloidal oncotic pressure = 7* In this question, - GFR, 3 = Kf Kf Kf . - GC = 27 mm/Hg."
                ],
                "answer": [
                    "27"
                ],
                "generated_cot": [
                    {
                        "id": "c538668c-61ff-4148-b46b-e50d89280f84",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nD) 27",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "a3e4defc-bddb-437b-9041-4d27382de654",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": " D.\n\nThe net filtration pressure is equal to the difference between the capillary hydrostatic pressure and the sum of the interstitial hydrostatic pressure and the interstitial oncotic pressure.\n\nNet filtration pressure = Capillary hydrostatic pressure - (Interstitial hydrostatic pressure + Interstitial oncotic pressure)\n\nNet filtration pressure = 25 mm Hg - (2 mm Hg + 7 mm Hg)\n\nNet filtration pressure = 16 mm Hg\n\nTherefore, the capillary oncotic pressure must be equal to the net filtration pressure plus the sum of the interstitial hydrostatic pressure and the interstitial oncotic pressure.\n\nCapillary oncotic pressure = Net filtration pressure + (Interstitial hydrostatic pressure + Interstitial oncotic pressure)\n\nCapillary oncotic pressure = 16 mm Hg + (2 mm Hg + 7 mm Hg)\n\nCapillary oncotic pressure = 25 mm Hg + 7 mm Hg\n\nCapillary oncotic pressure = 27 mm Hg",
                                "answer_from_choices": "D",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
                        "date": "2023/02/16 19:01:45",
                        "api_service": "openai",
                        "model": "{'name': 'text-davinci-003', 'temperature': 0, 'max_tokens': 512}",
                        "comment": "",
                        "annotations": []
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "4eae8a16-8242-4724-9db7-15a1e8735577",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": " Net filtration pressure is the difference between the hydrostatic pressure and the oncotic pressure. So, we need to find the oncotic pressure that will give us a difference of 3 mm Hg. \n\nCapillary hydrostatic pressure (25 mm Hg) - Interstitial oncotic pressure (7 mm Hg) = 18 mm Hg\n\n18 mm Hg - Net filtration pressure (3 mm Hg) = 15 mm Hg\n\nTherefore, the capillary oncotic pressure should be 15 mm Hg. Answer: D) 27",
                        "answers": [
