                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Net filtration pressure is the difference between the forces that promote filtration (capillary hydrostatic pressure and interstitial oncotic pressure) and the forces that oppose filtration (interstitial hydrostatic pressure and capillary oncotic pressure). \n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the equation for net filtration pressure?\n2. What are the values of the forces that promote filtration?\n3. What are the values of the forces that oppose filtration?\n4. What is the unknown value of capillary oncotic pressure?\n5. How can we rearrange the equation for net filtration pressure to solve for capillary oncotic pressure?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Net filtration pressure = (capillary hydrostatic pressure + interstitial oncotic pressure) - (interstitial hydrostatic pressure + capillary oncotic pressure)\n2. Capillary hydrostatic pressure = 25 mm Hg, interstitial oncotic pressure = 7 mm Hg\n3. Interstitial hydrostatic pressure = 2 mm Hg, capillary oncotic pressure = unknown\n4. Capillary oncotic pressure = ?\n5. Rearranging the equation for net filtration pressure: capillary oncotic pressure = (capillary hydrostatic pressure + interstitial oncotic pressure) - (interstitial hydrostatic pressure + net filtration pressure)\n\nUsing the values given in the question, we can plug them into the rearranged equation:\ncapillary oncotic pressure = (25 mm Hg + 7 mm Hg) - (2 mm Hg + 3 mm Hg)\ncapillary oncotic pressure = 29 mm Hg - 5 mm Hg\ncapillary oncotic pressure = 24 mm Hg\n\nTherefore, the answer is C) 23.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "b1a76bf8-8fcc-4355-a351-a4778bd77039",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
