                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The question is asking what should be done with an IOL (intraocular lens) that was placed in a young male after 10 years. \n\nFirst, it is important to understand what an IOL is and why it may need to be removed or changed. An IOL is a small artificial lens that is implanted in the eye to replace the natural lens that has been removed during cataract surgery. Over time, the capsule that holds the IOL in place may become cloudy or develop scar tissue, which can affect vision. This is known as posterior capsule ossification.\n\nOption A suggests that the IOL should be removed in case of posterior capsule ossification. This may be necessary if the cloudiness or scar tissue is severe enough to significantly affect vision. However, it is not always necessary to remove the IOL in these cases, as there are other treatments that can be used to improve vision.\n\nOption B suggests that the IOL should never be removed. This is not necessarily true, as there may be situations where the IOL needs to be removed or replaced for medical reasons.\n\nOption C suggests that the IOL should be removed when presbyopia sets in. Presbyopia is a condition that affects near vision and typically occurs in middle age. However, this is not a reason to remove an IOL, as presbyopia can be corrected with glasses or contact lenses.\n\nOption D suggests that the IOL should be changed after 10 years. This may be necessary if the IOL is no longer providing clear vision or if there are other medical reasons to replace it. However, it is not always necessary to change the IOL after 10 years, as some IOLs can last for many years without needing to be replaced.\n\nBased on this information, the most appropriate answer to the question is A) IOL should be removed in case of Posterior Capsule ossification, but it is important to note that this may not always be necessary and other factors may need to be considered before making a decision about removing or changing an IOL.",
