                        "cot": " \n\nThe cortex of the lymph node contains the lymphatic nodules. Lymphatic nodules are small, round, or ovoid bodies that are arranged in a diffuse or follicular pattern. The nodules are composed of a central mass of lymphocytes surrounded by a mantle of epithelial cells.\n\nThe cortex also contains Hassall's corpuscles. Hassall's corpuscles are spherical bodies that are located in the deep cortex. The corpuscles are composed of a central mass of lymphocytes surrounded by a mantle of epithelial cells.\n\nThe cortex also contains cords of Billroth. Cords of Billroth are linear arrays of lymphocytes that are located in the deep cortex. The cords are composed of a central mass of lymphocytes surrounded by a mantle of epithelial cells.\n\nFinally, the cortex also contains white pulp. White pulp is a diffuse aggregate of lymphocytes that is located in the deep cortex. The white pulp is composed of a central mass of lymphocytes surrounded by a mantle of epithelial cells.",
