                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\n- Excretion refers to the removal of a drug from the body, usually through urine or feces. This occurs after the drug has entered the systemic circulation and has been metabolized by the liver or other organs. Therefore, option A is not correct.\n- Metabolism refers to the chemical transformation of a drug by enzymes in the body, usually in the liver. This can occur both before and after the drug enters the systemic circulation. Therefore, option D is not specific enough to describe the process in question.\n- First order elimination refers to the exponential decrease in drug concentration in the body over time, which is characteristic of most drugs. This occurs after the drug has entered the systemic circulation and is being eliminated by the body. Therefore, option C is not correct.\n- The correct answer is B) First pass effect. This refers to the rapid metabolism and elimination of a drug by the liver and other organs before it enters the systemic circulation. This can significantly reduce the amount of drug that reaches the target site and can affect the drug's efficacy and safety. The first pass effect is particularly important for drugs that are administered orally, as they must pass through the liver before reaching the rest of the body.",
