                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "A) Active surveillance: This involves actively seeking out and monitoring cases of post-operative vision effects after cataract extraction surgery under PCB. This could involve regular follow-up appointments or phone calls to check on the patient's vision. This could be a possible option for evaluating post-operative vision effects, but it is not the most commonly used method.\n\nB) Sentinel surveillance: This involves monitoring a specific group of patients who are considered to be at high risk for post-operative vision effects after cataract extraction surgery under PCB. This could be a possible option for evaluating post-operative vision effects, but it is not the most commonly used method.\n\nC) Passive surveillance: This involves relying on patients or healthcare providers to report cases of post-operative vision effects after cataract extraction surgery under PCB. This is the most commonly used method for evaluating post-operative vision effects.\n\nD) Routine checkup of all operated cases: This involves scheduling regular checkups for all patients who have undergone cataract extraction surgery under PCB, regardless of whether they are experiencing any post-operative vision effects. This could be a possible option for evaluating post-operative vision effects, but it is not the most commonly used method.\n\nCombining all the information gathered, the most commonly used method for evaluating post-operative vision effects after cataract extraction surgery under PCB is passive surveillance, which involves relying on patients or healthcare providers to report cases of post-operative vision effects.\n\nTherefore, the answer is C) Passive surveillance.",
