                        "cot": "The correct answer is D) Routine checkup of all operated cases.\n\nTo evaluate post-operative vision effects after cataract extraction surgery under PCB, the most appropriate method would be to conduct routine checkups of all operated cases. This would involve scheduling follow-up appointments with patients after their surgery to assess their vision and ensure that there are no complications or adverse effects. \n\nActive surveillance involves actively seeking out cases of a particular condition or disease, which is not necessary in this case as patients who have undergone cataract extraction surgery are already known. Sentinel surveillance involves monitoring a specific group of people who are at high risk for a particular condition, which is also not necessary in this case as cataract extraction surgery is a common procedure with a known risk profile. Passive surveillance involves relying on healthcare providers to report cases of a particular condition, which is not appropriate in this case as routine checkups would provide more comprehensive and timely information about post-operative vision effects. \n\nTherefore, the most appropriate method to evaluate post-operative vision effects after cataract extraction surgery under PCB is routine checkup of all operated cases.",
