                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nDifficult. \n\nThe question is asking about the method used to evaluate post-operative vision effects after cataract extraction surgery under PCB. To answer this question, we need to understand what each of the options means and which one is relevant to the given scenario.\n\nA) Active surveillance: This refers to a method of monitoring a disease or condition by actively seeking out cases through various means, such as contacting healthcare providers or conducting surveys. This may not be relevant to evaluating post-operative vision effects.\n\nB) Sentinel surveillance: This refers to a method of monitoring a disease or condition by focusing on a specific group or population that is considered to be at high risk. This may not be relevant to evaluating post-operative vision effects.\n\nC) Passive surveillance: This refers to a method of monitoring a disease or condition by relying on healthcare providers to report cases to public health authorities. This may be relevant to evaluating post-operative vision effects, as healthcare providers may report any vision-related complications or issues.\n\nD) Routine checkup of all operated cases: This refers to a method of monitoring patients after surgery by scheduling regular follow-up appointments to assess their progress and identify any complications. This is likely to be relevant to evaluating post-operative vision effects.\n\nTherefore, the answer is D) Routine checkup of all operated cases.",
