                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Understand X-Linked SCID\nX-Linked SCID is a severe combined immunodeficiency disorder caused by mutations in the IL2RG gene, which is located on the X chromosome. This gene provides instructions for making a protein called the common gamma chain, which is a component of several receptors on immune cells. Without a functional common gamma chain, the immune system cannot develop properly, leading to severe infections and other health problems.\n\nStep 2: Identify the genes involved\nA) EGFR: This gene encodes the epidermal growth factor receptor, which is involved in cell growth and division. It is not directly related to immune function or X-Linked SCID.\nB) CD23: This gene encodes a protein called the low-affinity immunoglobulin E receptor, which is involved in allergic reactions and immune regulation. It is not directly related to X-Linked SCID.\nC) IL6: This gene encodes interleukin-6, a cytokine that is involved in inflammation and immune responses. While IL-6 is important for immune function, it is not directly related to X-Linked SCID.\nD) IL2RG: This gene encodes the common gamma chain, which is essential for immune cell development and function. Mutations in this gene cause X-Linked SCID.\n\nAnswer: D) IL2RG is the gene involved in X-Linked SCID.",
