                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nIntracellular killing of microbes refers to the process by which immune cells, such as macrophages and neutrophils, engulf and destroy invading pathogens within their own cytoplasm. This process involves the production of reactive oxygen species (ROS) and reactive nitrogen species (RNS), which are highly toxic to microbes.\n\nOne of the key enzymes involved in the production of ROS is oxidase, which catalyzes the conversion of oxygen to superoxide anion (O2-). This reactive molecule can then be converted to other ROS, such as hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) and hydroxyl radical (OH-), which can damage microbial membranes and DNA.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) Oxidase.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "8b861ee7-015e-4540-88f0-0add141794b0",
