                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "A) Catalase: Catalase is an enzyme that breaks down hydrogen peroxide into water and oxygen. It is not directly involved in intracellular killing of microbes.\n\nB) Oxidase: Oxidase is an enzyme that catalyzes the transfer of electrons from a donor molecule to an acceptor molecule, often involving the reduction of oxygen to produce hydrogen peroxide. While hydrogen peroxide can be involved in intracellular killing of microbes, oxidase itself is not directly involved.\n\nC) Lysozyme: Lysozyme is an enzyme that breaks down bacterial cell walls by cleaving the bonds between the sugar molecules in the peptidoglycan layer. This can contribute to intracellular killing of microbes by breaking down bacterial cells.\n\nD) IL6: Interleukin-6 (IL6) is a cytokine that plays a role in inflammation and immune response, but it is not directly involved in intracellular killing of microbes.\n\nCombining all the information, the correct answer is C) Lysozyme.",
