                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Intracellular killing of microbes is a process by which immune cells, such as macrophages and neutrophils, engulf and destroy invading microorganisms. This process involves the production of reactive oxygen species (ROS) and reactive nitrogen species (RNS) that are toxic to the microbes.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What are inflammatory mediators?\n2. What is the role of ROS and RNS in intracellular killing of microbes?\n3. Which inflammatory mediators are involved in the production of ROS and RNS?\n4. Which of the given options (A, B, C, D) are inflammatory mediators that can produce ROS and RNS?\n\nAnswer:\n1. Inflammatory mediators are molecules that are produced by immune cells in response to infection, injury, or other stimuli. They play a crucial role in the initiation and regulation of the immune response.\n2. ROS and RNS are produced by immune cells during intracellular killing of microbes. They are highly reactive molecules that can damage the DNA, proteins, and lipids of the invading microorganisms, leading to their destruction.\n3. Inflammatory mediators such as cytokines, chemokines, and growth factors can stimulate the production of ROS and RNS by immune cells.\n4. Option B (Oxidase) is an inflammatory mediator that can produce ROS during intracellular killing of microbes. Therefore, the answer to the original question is B) Oxidase. Options A (Catalase), C (Lysozyme), and D (IL6) are not inflammatory mediators that are involved in the production of ROS and RNS during intracellular killing of microbes.",
