                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nB) Vestibular neurectomy is the best surgery to relieve intractable vertigo in a Meniere's disease patient. This surgery involves cutting the vestibular nerve, which is responsible for transmitting balance information from the inner ear to the brain. By cutting this nerve, the patient's vertigo symptoms can be significantly reduced or eliminated. Other surgeries, such as surgical labyrinthectomy, endolymphatic sac decompression, and cochleosacculotomy, may also be used to treat Meniere's disease, but they are not as effective at relieving vertigo symptoms.",
