                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Understand the problem\nThe question is asking about the best surgery to relieve intractable vertigo in a patient with Meniere's disease. Meniere's disease is a disorder of the inner ear that causes episodes of vertigo, hearing loss, tinnitus, and a feeling of fullness in the ear.\n\nStep 2: Understand the options\nThe question provides four options for surgery:\nA) Surgical Labyrinthectomy: This surgery involves removing the entire inner ear, including the balance and hearing organs. It is typically reserved for severe cases of Meniere's disease that do not respond to other treatments.\nB) Vestibular neurectomy: This surgery involves cutting the vestibular nerve, which carries balance information from the inner ear to the brain. By cutting this nerve, the brain no longer receives signals of vertigo from the affected ear.\nC) Endolymphatic sac decompression: This surgery involves opening the endolymphatic sac, which is a small pouch in the inner ear that regulates fluid balance. By opening the sac, excess fluid can drain, which can reduce the frequency and severity of vertigo attacks.\nD) Cochleosacculotomy: This surgery involves making a small hole in the sacculus, which is a small pouch in the inner ear that is involved in balance and hearing. By making this hole, pressure can be relieved, which can reduce vertigo attacks.\n\nStep 3: Evaluate the options\nAll of the surgeries listed have been used to treat intractable vertigo in Meniere's disease patients, but some are more effective than others. \n\nSurgical Labyrinthectomy is a drastic option that is typically only used in severe cases. It is effective at eliminating vertigo, but it also results in complete hearing loss in the affected ear.\n\nVestibular neurectomy is a less invasive option that can be effective at reducing vertigo attacks. However, it also results in complete loss of balance function in the affected ear.\n\nEndolymphatic sac decompression is a minimally invasive option that can be effective at reducing the frequency and severity of vertigo attacks. It does not result in hearing loss or loss of balance function.\n\nCochleosacculotomy is a newer procedure that has shown promise in reducing vertigo attacks. It is less invasive than labyrinthectomy and does not result in complete hearing loss or loss of balance function.\n\nStep 4: Choose the best option\nBased on the evaluation of the options, the best surgery to relieve intract",
