                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step-by-step procedure:\n1. Understand what intractable vertigo is and how it relates to Meniere's disease.\n2. Research the different surgical options available for treating intractable vertigo in Meniere's disease patients.\n3. Evaluate the effectiveness and potential risks of each surgical option.\n4. Determine which surgical option is the best choice for relieving intractable vertigo in Meniere's disease patients.\n\nGoing through each step:\n1. Intractable vertigo is a type of vertigo that is severe and difficult to control. It is a common symptom of Meniere's disease, which is a disorder of the inner ear that affects balance and hearing.\n2. The four surgical options available for treating intractable vertigo in Meniere's disease patients are surgical labyrinthectomy, vestibular neurectomy, endolymphatic sac decompression, and cochleosacculotomy.\n3. According to research, surgical labyrinthectomy and vestibular neurectomy are the most effective options for relieving intractable vertigo in Meniere's disease patients. However, both procedures carry a risk of hearing loss and other complications. Endolymphatic sac decompression and cochleosacculotomy are less invasive options, but their effectiveness is not as well-established.\n4. Based on the research, the best surgical option for relieving intractable vertigo in Meniere's disease patients is either surgical labyrinthectomy or vestibular neurectomy, depending on the individual patient's needs and risks.",
                        "answers": [
