                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "A) Surgical Labyrinthectomy: This surgery involves removing the entire inner ear, including the balance and hearing organs. While it can effectively relieve vertigo, it also results in permanent hearing loss. Therefore, it may not be the best option for a Meniere's disease patient who still has some hearing ability.\n\nB) Vestibular neurectomy: This surgery involves cutting the vestibular nerve, which carries balance information from the inner ear to the brain. By severing this nerve, the brain no longer receives signals that cause vertigo. This surgery is effective in relieving vertigo, but it can also result in some hearing loss.\n\nC) Endolymphatic sac decompression: This surgery involves creating a small hole in the endolymphatic sac, which is a structure in the inner ear that regulates fluid balance. By reducing the pressure in the inner ear, this surgery can help relieve vertigo. It is a less invasive option than labyrinthectomy or vestibular neurectomy and does not result in hearing loss.\n\nD) Cochleosacculotomy: This surgery involves making a small hole in the sacculus, which is a structure in the inner ear that is involved in balance and hearing. By reducing the pressure in the sacculus, this surgery can help relieve vertigo. However, it is a less common procedure and may not be as effective as other options.\n\nBased on the information provided, the best surgery to relieve intractable vertigo in a Meniere's disease patient would be C) Endolymphatic sac decompression. It is a less invasive option than labyrinthectomy or vestibular neurectomy and does not result in hearing loss.",
                        "answers": [
