                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Meniere's disease is a disorder of the inner ear that causes vertigo, hearing loss, tinnitus, and a feeling of fullness or pressure in the ear. Intractable vertigo is a severe form of vertigo that is difficult to control with medication or other treatments. Surgery may be necessary to relieve intractable vertigo in Meniere's disease patients. \n\nA) Surgical Labyrinthectomy involves removing the entire inner ear, including the balance and hearing organs. This surgery is usually reserved for patients with severe and disabling vertigo that cannot be controlled with other treatments. However, it also results in complete hearing loss in the affected ear.\n\nB) Vestibular neurectomy involves cutting the vestibular nerve, which carries balance information from the inner ear to the brain. This surgery can be effective in relieving vertigo, but it also results in complete hearing loss in the affected ear.\n\nC) Endolymphatic sac decompression involves opening the endolymphatic sac, which is a small pouch in the inner ear that regulates fluid balance. This surgery is thought to relieve pressure in the inner ear and reduce vertigo attacks. It may also preserve hearing in some cases.\n\nD) Cochleosacculotomy involves opening the sacculus, which is a small pouch in the inner ear that is involved in balance and hearing. This surgery is thought to relieve pressure in the inner ear and reduce vertigo attacks. It may also preserve hearing in some cases.\n\nBased on the above information, the best surgery to relieve intractable vertigo in a Meniere's disease patient depends on the severity of the vertigo and the patient's hearing status. Surgical Labyrinthectomy and Vestibular neurectomy are more invasive surgeries that result in complete hearing loss, and are usually reserved for severe cases. Endolymphatic sac decompression and Cochleosacculotomy are less invasive surgeries that may preserve hearing in some cases, and are often considered first-line treatments for intractable vertigo in Meniere's disease patients. Therefore, the best surgery to relieve intractable vertigo in a Meniere's disease patient is either C) Endolymphatic sac decompression or D) Cochleosacculotomy, depending on the patient's individual case.",
