                    {
                        "id": "63965054-e26e-4bd3-92bf-2d51e5af12bd",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Meniere's disease is a disorder of the inner ear that causes episodes of vertigo, tinnitus, and hearing loss. Intractable vertigo is a severe form of vertigo that is difficult to control with medication or other treatments.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is surgical labyrinthectomy?\n2. How does vestibular neurectomy work?\n3. What is endolymphatic sac decompression?\n4. What is cochleosacculotomy?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Surgical labyrinthectomy is a procedure in which the inner ear is surgically removed to eliminate vertigo. This procedure is usually reserved for patients with severe, intractable vertigo who have not responded to other treatments.\n2. Vestibular neurectomy is a procedure in which the vestibular nerve, which carries signals from the inner ear to the brain, is surgically cut. This procedure can be effective in relieving vertigo, but it can also cause hearing loss.\n3. Endolymphatic sac decompression is a procedure in which a small opening is made in the endolymphatic sac, a structure in the inner ear that regulates fluid balance. This procedure can help to reduce the frequency and severity of vertigo attacks.\n4. Cochleosacculotomy is a procedure in which a small hole is made in the sacculus, a structure in the inner ear that is involved in balance and hearing. This procedure can help to reduce vertigo and improve hearing, but it is not commonly used.\n\nBased on the information above, the best surgery to relieve intractable vertigo in a Meniere's disease patient would likely be either surgical labyrinthectomy or vestibular neurectomy, depending on the individual patient's specific circumstances and preferences. Endolymphatic sac decompression may also be a viable option for some patients. Cochleosacculotomy is not typically used for this purpose. Ultimately, the decision about which surgery to pursue should be made in consultation with a qualified healthcare provider.",
