                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, what is intractable veigo?\n\nProfessor: Intractable veigo is a type of vertigo that is difficult to treat and can be caused by Meniere's disease.\n\nStudent: What is Meniere's disease?\n\nProfessor: Meniere's disease is a disorder of the inner ear that can cause vertigo, hearing loss, and ringing in the ears.\n\nStudent: Okay, so what is the best surgery to relieve intractable veigo in a Meniere's disease patient?\n\nProfessor: The best surgery for this is either a surgical labyrinthectomy, a vestibular neurectomy, an endolymphatic sac decompression, or a cochleosacculotomy.\n\nStudent: Can you explain each of these surgeries?\n\nProfessor: Sure. A surgical labyrinthectomy involves removing the entire inner ear, which eliminates the source of vertigo. A vestibular neurectomy involves cutting the nerve that sends signals from the inner ear to the brain, which also eliminates vertigo. An endolymphatic sac decompression involves opening up the endolymphatic sac in the inner ear to relieve pressure and reduce vertigo. Finally, a cochleosacculotomy involves removing a small part of the inner ear to reduce vertigo.\n\nStudent: Which surgery is the most effective?\n\nProfessor: It depends on the individual patient and their specific case. Each surgery has its own risks and benefits, and the decision should be made in consultation with a doctor who specializes in Meniere's disease.",
