                        "cot": "\n\nThe test involves placing one hand under the patient's heel and asking them to raise their other leg against downward resistance. This suggests that the test is assessing the strength of the patient's lower limbs. \n\nOption A, the Hoover test, is a test used to assess for malingering or non-organic causes of low back pain. It involves assessing for bilateral leg weakness by checking for downward pressure on the heels while the patient lifts their legs. This is not the test being described in the question. \n\nOption B, Waddell's test, is a series of tests used to assess for non-organic causes of low back pain. It includes tests for tenderness, simulation, distraction, regional disturbances, and overreaction. This is not the test being described in the question. \n\nOption C, the O'Donoghue test, is a test used to assess for ligamentous injuries in the knee. It involves assessing for pain and instability in the knee joint with various movements. This is not the test being described in the question. \n\nOption D, the McBride test, is not a commonly known test in medical literature. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A) Hoover test.",
