                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "A) Hoover test: This test is used to assess for malingering or non-organic causes of low back pain. It involves the examiner placing their hands under the patient's heels and asking them to lift one leg while keeping the other leg straight. The examiner can feel for any weight shift or lack of effort in the non-lifting leg. This test is not used to assess lower limb weakness.\n\nB) Waddell's test: This test is also used to assess for non-organic causes of low back pain. It involves the examiner applying pressure to certain areas of the body to see if the patient reports pain in a way that is inconsistent with a true physical injury. This test is not used to assess lower limb weakness.\n\nC) O'Donoghue test: This test is used to assess for ligamentous injuries in the knee. It involves the patient performing three movements: passive flexion, active flexion, and resisted extension. This test is not used to assess lower limb weakness.\n\nD) McBride test: This test is used to assess for instability in the ankle joint. It involves the patient standing on one leg while the examiner applies pressure to the outside of the ankle. This test is not used to assess lower limb weakness.\n\nBased on the clues given, the correct answer is A) Hoover test.",
