                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "What is the name of the test where the examiner places one hand under the patient's heel and the patient is asked to raise their other leg against downward resistance due to lower limb weakness?\n\nTo answer this question, we need to identify the specific test being described and its name. Here are our thoughts and reasoning:\n\n- The test involves the examiner placing one hand under the patient's heel, which suggests that the focus is on the lower limbs.\n- The patient is then asked to raise their other leg against downward resistance, which could be a way to assess the strength of the lower limbs.\n- The fact that the patient has complaints of lower limb weakness also supports the idea that this is a strength test.\n- Based on these clues, we can eliminate options B, C, and D, as they are not specifically related to lower limb strength testing.\n- That leaves us with option A, the Hoover test, which is a test used to assess lower limb strength by checking for voluntary effort in the contralateral limb. This involves the examiner placing one hand under the heel of the affected limb and the other hand under the heel of the contralateral limb, and asking the patient to lift the affected limb while applying downward pressure with the contralateral limb. If the patient is not exerting effort in the affected limb, the contralateral limb will not feel any pressure and may even rise slightly due to the lack of counterpressure.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A) Hoover test.",
