                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "we need to understand the different schedules of drugs in India. The schedules are based on the potential for abuse and the level of control required for their distribution.\n\nSchedule H drugs are prescription drugs that can only be sold with a prescription from a registered medical practitioner. These drugs have a lower potential for abuse and include antibiotics, painkillers, and other commonly used medications.\n\nSchedule G drugs are over-the-counter drugs that can be sold without a prescription. These drugs have a low potential for abuse and include common medications like cough syrups, antacids, and vitamins.\n\nSchedule X drugs are highly addictive and have a high potential for abuse. These drugs can only be sold with a special license and strict controls. Examples include opioids, amphetamines, and barbiturates.\n\nSchedule M drugs are drugs that are manufactured for export and are subject to strict quality control measures.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A) Schedule H drugs must be sold only on production of a prescription by a registered medical practitioner.",
                        "answers": [
