                    {
                        "id": "f61f6919-5881-4e08-83bf-3694a874cce0",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: DNA and RNA are nucleic acids that are composed of nucleotides. The nucleotides have a sugar-phosphate backbone and a nitrogenous base. The nitrogenous bases in DNA are adenine (A), thymine (T), cytosine (C), and guanine (G). In RNA, uracil (U) replaces thymine. DNA replication is the process of copying DNA, while transcription is the process of making RNA from DNA. RNA editing is the process of altering the nucleotide sequence of RNA after transcription.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the 5' to 3' direction?\n2. Does DNA repair occur in the 5' to 3' direction?\n3. Does DNA replication occur in the 5' to 3' direction?\n4. Does RNA editing occur in the 5' to 3' direction?\n5. Does transcription occur in the 5' to 3' direction?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The 5' to 3' direction refers to the direction in which nucleotides are added to a growing nucleic acid chain. The 5' end has a phosphate group attached to the 5' carbon of the sugar, while the 3' end has a hydroxyl group attached to the 3' carbon of the sugar.\n2. DNA repair can occur in either direction since it involves the removal and replacement of damaged nucleotides.\n3. DNA replication occurs in the 5' to 3' direction since DNA polymerase can only add nucleotides to the 3' end of a growing strand.\n4. RNA editing can occur in either direction since it involves the alteration of nucleotides after transcription.\n5. Transcription occurs in the 5' to 3' direction since RNA polymerase adds nucleotides to the 3' end of the growing RNA strand. \n\nTherefore, the answer is C) RNA editing, which can occur in either direction and is not specific to the 5' to 3' direction.",
                        "answers": [
