                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe correct answer is A) 14-28 weeks.\n\nHere's why:\n\nChickenpox is a highly contagious viral infection caused by the varicella-zoster virus. It can be dangerous for pregnant women and their babies, especially if the mother contracts the infection during pregnancy.\n\nThe timing of the infection is crucial because it can affect the development of the baby's organs and cause congenital abnormalities. The risk of complications is highest during the first and second trimesters of pregnancy.\n\nOption B) 3-9 weeks is not a likely answer because this time frame is too early for the baby's organs to be fully developed. \n\nOption C) 28-37 weeks is also not the most dangerous time frame because the baby is close to full term and the risk of complications is lower.\n\nOption D) 8-14 weeks is also not the most dangerous time frame because the baby's organs are still developing, but not as rapidly as during the first trimester.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A) 14-28 weeks, which is the second trimester of pregnancy and the time when the baby's organs are rapidly developing. If the mother contracts chickenpox during this time, it can lead to serious complications such as limb abnormalities, eye and brain damage, and even fetal death.",
                        "answers": [
