                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Chickenpox infection in pregnant women can be dangerous for the baby, especially if the mother contracts the infection during certain periods of pregnancy. The risk to the baby depends on the timing of the infection.\n\nA) 14-28 weeks: If the mother contracts chickenpox during this period, there is a risk of the baby developing congenital varicella syndrome (CVS), which can cause birth defects such as scarring, limb abnormalities, and eye problems. However, the risk is relatively low, at around 1-2%.\n\nB) 3-9 weeks: If the mother contracts chickenpox during this period, the risk of CVS is slightly higher, at around 2-3%. However, this is still a relatively low risk.\n\nC) 28-37 weeks: If the mother contracts chickenpox during this period, the risk to the baby is different. The baby is less likely to develop CVS, but there is a risk of neonatal varicella, which can be a severe and life-threatening infection for the newborn. The risk is highest if the mother contracts chickenpox between 5 days before and 2 days after delivery.\n\nD) 8-14 weeks: This period falls within the range of 3-9 weeks, so the risk of CVS is similar, at around 2-3%.\n\nBased on this information, the most dangerous period for the baby is during 28-37 weeks (option C), due to the risk of neonatal varicella, which can be severe and life-threatening.",
