                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Chicken pox is a highly contagious viral infection caused by the varicella-zoster virus. It can cause serious complications in pregnant women and their babies, especially if the mother contracts the infection during pregnancy.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What are the risks of chicken pox infection during pregnancy?\n2. At what stage of pregnancy is the baby most vulnerable to complications from chicken pox?\n3. How can chicken pox infection during pregnancy be prevented and treated?\n\nAnswer:\n1. Chicken pox infection during pregnancy can increase the risk of complications such as pneumonia, encephalitis, and premature delivery. It can also cause birth defects, such as limb abnormalities, eye problems, and brain damage.\n2. According to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), the baby is most vulnerable to complications from chicken pox if the mother contracts the infection between 5 days before delivery and 2 days after delivery. This is because the baby may not have enough time to develop immunity to the virus before birth.\n3. Chicken pox infection during pregnancy can be prevented by getting vaccinated before pregnancy or avoiding contact with infected individuals. If a pregnant woman does contract chicken pox, she should seek medical attention immediately and may be given antiviral medication to reduce the severity of the infection. In some cases, the baby may need to be delivered early to prevent complications.",
