                        "cot": "\n\nC) FISH",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "dd5291d2-ae0e-4017-96f0-4db69c1632d7",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "C) FISH. FISH (fluorescence in situ hybridization) is a technique that uses fluorescent probes to detect specific DNA sequences. In Ewing's sarcoma, the translocation t(ll;22) is a characteristic genetic abnormality that results in the fusion of the EWSR1 gene on chromosome 22 with the FLI1 gene on chromosome 11. FISH can be used to detect this translocation by labeling the EWSR1 and FLI1 genes with different fluorescent probes and visualizing their fusion under a microscope. FISH is a highly sensitive and specific method for detecting this translocation in Ewing's sarcoma.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "C",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
                        "date": "2023/03/11 01:42:45",
