                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what translocation t(ll;22) means in this context?\n\nProfessor: Sure. Translocation t(ll;22) is a genetic abnormality that is commonly found in Ewing's sarcoma. It involves the fusion of two genes, EWSR1 and FLI1, which results in the formation of a chimeric protein that drives the growth of cancer cells.\n\nStudent: Okay, so how can we detect this translocation?\n\nProfessor: There are several methods that can be used, but the best one for this malignancy is FISH.\n\nStudent: What is FISH?\n\nProfessor: FISH stands for fluorescence in situ hybridization. It is a technique that uses fluorescent probes to bind to specific DNA sequences and visualize them under a microscope. In this case, we would use probes that target the EWSR1 and FLI1 genes to see if they have fused together.\n\nStudent: Why is FISH the best method for detecting this translocation?\n\nProfessor: FISH is highly sensitive and specific, meaning it can detect even small amounts of the translocation in a sample. It is also faster and more cost-effective than other methods like conventional karyotyping or next generation sequencing.\n\nStudent: Can you explain what those other methods are?\n\nProfessor: Conventional karyotyping involves analyzing the chromosomes of a cell to look for structural abnormalities like translocations. Next generation sequencing is a newer technique that can sequence large amounts of DNA quickly and accurately. However, both of these methods are more time-consuming and expensive than FISH, and may not be as sensitive for detecting this specific translocation.",
