                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nDifficult. The best method to detect translocation t(ll;22) in Ewing's sarcoma needs to be determined. \n\nEwing's sarcoma is characterized by a specific chromosomal translocation, t(ll;22), which results in the fusion of the EWSR1 gene on chromosome 22 with the FLI1 gene on chromosome 11. This translocation can be detected by various methods, including conventional karyotyping, fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH), polymerase chain reaction (PCR), and next-generation sequencing (NGS).\n\nConventional karyotyping involves the visualization of chromosomes under a microscope to identify structural abnormalities, including translocations. However, this method may not be sensitive enough to detect the t(ll;22) translocation in Ewing's sarcoma.\n\nNGS is a high-throughput sequencing technology that can detect genetic alterations, including translocations, with high sensitivity and specificity. However, it may not be readily available in all clinical settings.\n\nPCR is a sensitive and specific method for detecting specific DNA sequences, including the EWSR1-FLI1 fusion transcript resulting from the t(ll;22) translocation. However, it requires prior knowledge of the fusion transcript sequence.\n\nFISH is a technique that uses fluorescent probes to detect specific DNA sequences, including the EWSR1-FLI1 fusion gene resulting from the t(ll;22) translocation. It is a sensitive and specific method for detecting this translocation and is commonly used in clinical practice.\n\nTherefore, the best method to detect translocation t(ll;22) in Ewing's sarcoma is C) FISH.",
