                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Tetralogy of Fallot is a congenital heart defect that involves four abnormalities in the heart. These abnormalities include a ventricular septal defect, pulmonary stenosis, right ventricular hypertrophy, and an overriding aorta. Due to these abnormalities, the child may experience cyanosis (bluish discoloration of the skin) and difficulty breathing.\n\nTo alleviate these symptoms, the child may assume a position that helps increase blood flow to the lungs. The position that is commonly used for this purpose is squatting. Squatting helps increase systemic vascular resistance, which in turn decreases the amount of blood that is shunted from the right ventricle to the aorta. This helps increase blood flow to the lungs and improves oxygenation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C) Squatting.",
