                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Tetralogy of Fallot is a congenital heart defect that involves four abnormalities in the heart's structure. These abnormalities include a ventricular septal defect, pulmonary stenosis, right ventricular hypertrophy, and an overriding aorta. \n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the purpose of positioning for a child with tetralogy of Fallot?\n2. How does each position affect the child's heart and breathing?\n\nAnswer:\n1. The purpose of positioning for a child with tetralogy of Fallot is to improve blood flow and oxygenation to the body.\n2. The supine position (lying on their back) can cause blood to pool in the heart and decrease oxygenation. The prone position (lying on their stomach) can also decrease oxygenation. The squatting position can increase blood flow to the lungs and improve oxygenation. The leaning forward position can also improve blood flow to the lungs and decrease the workload on the heart. Therefore, the answer is D) Leaning forwards.",
