                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step-by-step procedure:\n1. Identify the condition: Excessive glycogen in muscle tissue causing pain on exercise.\n2. Recall the enzymes involved in glycogen metabolism: Glycogen synthase, glycogen phosphorylase, debranching enzyme, branching enzyme, and phosphoglucomutase.\n3. Recall the conditions associated with enzyme deficiencies: Glycogen storage diseases (GSDs).\n4. Match the symptoms with the GSDs: Pain on exercise is associated with GSD type V (McArdle disease) and type VII (Tarui disease).\n5. Recall the enzymes involved in GSD type V and type VII: GSD type V is caused by a deficiency in muscle phosphorylase enzyme, while GSD type VII is caused by a deficiency in muscle phosphofructokinase I enzyme.\n6. Choose the most likely enzyme deficiency based on the biopsy findings: Excessive glycogen in muscle tissue suggests a problem with glycogen breakdown, which is primarily regulated by muscle phosphorylase enzyme. Therefore, the most likely enzyme deficiency is D) Phosphorylase enzyme.\n\nGoing through each step:\n1. The condition is excessive glycogen in muscle tissue causing pain on exercise.\n2. The enzymes involved in glycogen metabolism are recalled.\n3. The conditions associated with enzyme deficiencies are recalled.\n4. Pain on exercise is matched with GSD type V and type VII.\n5. The enzymes involved in GSD type V and type VII are recalled.\n6. The most likely enzyme deficiency is chosen based on the biopsy findings, which suggest a problem with glycogen breakdown, primarily regulated by muscle phosphorylase enzyme. Therefore, the answer is D) Phosphorylase enzyme.",
