                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "A) Muscle debranching enzyme: This enzyme is responsible for breaking down glycogen into glucose. Deficiency of this enzyme would lead to accumulation of glycogen in the muscle, but it would not cause pain on exercise. Therefore, this option is unlikely to be correct.\n\nB) Phosphofructokinase I: This enzyme is involved in the glycolytic pathway and deficiency of this enzyme can lead to exercise intolerance and muscle pain. However, it would not cause excessive glycogen accumulation in the muscle. Therefore, this option is unlikely to be correct.\n\nC) Glucose 6 phosphatase: This enzyme is involved in gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis. Deficiency of this enzyme can lead to glycogen accumulation in the liver, but it would not cause pain in the calf muscles on exercise. Therefore, this option is unlikely to be correct.\n\nD) Phosphorylase enzyme: This enzyme is responsible for breaking down glycogen into glucose in muscle tissue. Deficiency of this enzyme can lead to excessive glycogen accumulation in the muscle, which can cause pain on exercise. Therefore, this option is the most likely enzyme deficiency in this case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D) Phosphorylase enzyme.",
