                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Read the question and identify the key information.\n- Adolescent male patient with pain in calf muscles on exercise\n- Biopsy showed excessive glycogen in the muscle\n- Which enzyme deficiency is most likely?\n\nStep 2: Recall the pathway of glycogen metabolism.\n- Glycogen is broken down into glucose-1-phosphate by the enzyme glycogen phosphorylase.\n- Glucose-1-phosphate is converted to glucose-6-phosphate by the enzyme phosphoglucomutase.\n- Glucose-6-phosphate can either be used for energy production or stored as glycogen.\n- Glycogen synthesis requires the enzymes glycogen synthase and branching enzyme.\n\nStep 3: Consider the options and eliminate unlikely answers.\nA) Muscle debranching enzyme: This enzyme is involved in glycogen breakdown and synthesis, but a deficiency would result in a different type of glycogen storage disease (cori disease or andersen disease).\nB) Phosphofructokinase I: This enzyme is involved in glycolysis, not glycogen metabolism.\nC) Glucose 6 phosphatase: This enzyme is involved in gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis, but a deficiency would result in von Gierke disease, not excessive glycogen storage in muscle.\nD) Phosphorylase enzyme: This enzyme is responsible for breaking down glycogen into glucose-1-phosphate, so a deficiency would result in glycogen storage disease type V (McArdle disease). However, this is not the most likely answer for the given symptoms.\n\nStep 4: Choose the best answer.\nThe most likely enzyme deficiency in this case is glycogen synthase or branching enzyme, which are involved in glycogen synthesis. However, neither of these options are listed. Therefore, the best answer among the given options is A) Muscle debranching enzyme, which is also involved in glycogen synthesis and breakdown. A deficiency in this enzyme can result in a type III glycogen storage disease (Forbes disease), which can cause muscle pain and weakness during exercise.",
                        "answers": [
