                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the key information in the question.\n\n- Adolescent male patient\n- Pain in calf muscles on exercise\n- Excessive amount of glycogen present in muscle on biopsy\n\nStep 2: Recall the pathway of glycogen metabolism.\n\n- Glycogen is broken down into glucose-1-phosphate by the enzyme glycogen phosphorylase.\n- Glucose-1-phosphate is converted to glucose-6-phosphate by the enzyme phosphoglucomutase.\n- Glucose-6-phosphate can either be used for energy production or stored as glycogen.\n- Glycogen synthesis is catalyzed by the enzymes glycogen synthase and branching enzyme.\n\nStep 3: Consider the possible enzyme deficiencies.\n\n- Muscle debranching enzyme deficiency: This would result in the accumulation of glycogen with long outer branches, leading to a condition called glycogen storage disease type III (also known as Cori disease). However, this condition typically presents with hepatomegaly (enlarged liver) and hypoglycemia (low blood sugar), not muscle pain.\n- Phosphofructokinase I deficiency: This enzyme is involved in glycolysis, not glycogen metabolism. Deficiency of this enzyme would result in exercise intolerance and muscle pain, but not glycogen accumulation.\n- Glucose 6 phosphatase deficiency: This enzyme is involved in gluconeogenesis (the production of glucose from non-carbohydrate sources), not glycogen metabolism. Deficiency of this enzyme would result in hypoglycemia, not muscle pain.\n- Phosphorylase enzyme deficiency: This enzyme is responsible for breaking down glycogen into glucose-1-phosphate. Deficiency of this enzyme would result in the accumulation of glycogen in muscle and liver, leading to a condition called glycogen storage disease type V (also known as McArdle disease). This condition typically presents with exercise intolerance, muscle pain, and myoglobinuria (the presence of myoglobin in the urine).\n\nStep 4: Choose the most likely enzyme deficiency based on the information in the question.\n\n- The fact that the patient has pain in calf muscles on exercise suggests a problem with energy production in muscle.\n- The presence of excessive glycogen in muscle on biopsy suggests a problem with glycogen metabolism.\n- The most likely enzyme deficiency that would result in these symptoms is phosphorylase enzyme deficiency (choice D).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D) Phosphorylase enzyme.",
