                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe positive pivot shift test is a clinical test used to diagnose knee instability. It is performed by flexing the knee to 90 degrees and applying a valgus force while internally rotating the tibia. If there is a sudden shift or clunk felt as the knee is extended, it is considered a positive test.\n\nNow, let's consider the options given:\n\nA) Posterior cruciate ligament (PCL) - The PCL is responsible for preventing posterior translation of the tibia on the femur. Injury to the PCL can cause posterior sag of the tibia, but it is not typically associated with a positive pivot shift test.\n\nB) Anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) - The ACL is responsible for preventing anterior translation of the tibia on the femur. Injury to the ACL can cause a positive pivot shift test, as the tibia will shift forward as the knee is extended.\n\nC) Medial meniscus - The medial meniscus is a C-shaped cartilage that sits between the tibia and femur. Injury to the medial meniscus can cause pain, swelling, and clicking or locking of the knee, but it is not typically associated with a positive pivot shift test.\n\nD) Lateral meniscus - The lateral meniscus is also a C-shaped cartilage that sits between the tibia and femur. Injury to the lateral meniscus can cause pain, swelling, and clicking or locking of the knee, but it is not typically associated with a positive pivot shift test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) Anterior cruciate ligament (ACL).",
                        "answers": [
