                        "instruction": "qa-09",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step-by-step procedure:\n1. Understand what the pivot shift test is and how it is performed.\n2. Identify the structures in the knee that are involved in the pivot shift test.\n3. Determine which of these structures is likely to be injured if the test is positive.\n4. Choose the answer option that corresponds to the injured structure.\n\nGoing through each step:\n1. The pivot shift test is a physical examination maneuver used to assess the stability of the knee joint. It involves flexing the knee, applying a valgus force (pushing the knee inward), and then rotating the tibia (lower leg bone) while extending the knee. If the test is positive, there is a sudden shift or clunk felt as the tibia subluxates (partially dislocates) and then reduces (returns to its normal position) as the knee is extended.\n2. The structures involved in the pivot shift test are the anterior cruciate ligament (ACL), lateral meniscus, and lateral collateral ligament (LCL).\n3. The ACL is the most commonly injured structure in a positive pivot shift test. When the ACL is torn, the tibia can subluxate forward and rotate abnormally, leading to the characteristic clunk or shift felt during the test. In contrast, injuries to the lateral meniscus or LCL are less likely to cause a positive pivot shift test.\n4. Therefore, the correct answer is B) Anterior cruciate ligament.",
                        "answers": [
