                    {
                        "id": "3050f2c2-da67-441d-88d5-60e6fdb70791",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-17",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what the positive pivot shift test is?\n\nProfessor: Sure, the pivot shift test is a physical exam maneuver used to assess the stability of the knee joint. A positive test means that there is an abnormal movement of the tibia bone in relation to the femur bone, indicating a possible ligament or meniscus injury.\n\nStudent: Okay, so what does it mean if the test is positive?\n\nProfessor: If the test is positive, it means that there is an injury to one or more of the structures that help stabilize the knee joint. These structures include the anterior cruciate ligament (ACL), posterior cruciate ligament (PCL), medial meniscus, and lateral meniscus.\n\nStudent: How do we know which structure is injured?\n\nProfessor: Based on the location of the pain and the specific movements that cause pain, we can narrow down which structure is most likely injured. For example, if the pain is on the inside of the knee and is worse when the knee is bent, it may indicate an injury to the medial meniscus.\n\nStudent: So, which structure is most likely injured if the pivot shift test is positive?\n\nProfessor: In the case of a positive pivot shift test, the most likely structure to be injured is the anterior cruciate ligament (ACL). However, it is important to do further testing and imaging to confirm the diagnosis and determine the extent of the injury.",
