                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Understand the pivot shift test\nThe pivot shift test is a physical examination test used to diagnose knee injuries, particularly those involving the ligaments and menisci. The test involves the examiner applying a valgus (inward) force to the knee while it is flexed, followed by a sudden extension of the knee. The test is considered positive if there is a sudden shift or clunk felt or heard as the knee is extended.\n\nStep 2: Identify the possible causes of a positive pivot shift test\nA positive pivot shift test can be caused by injuries to several structures in the knee, including the anterior cruciate ligament (ACL), posterior cruciate ligament (PCL), medial collateral ligament (MCL), lateral collateral ligament (LCL), and the medial and lateral menisci.\n\nStep 3: Eliminate the unlikely options\nSince the pivot shift test involves a valgus force, we can eliminate the lateral collateral ligament (LCL) as a possible cause of a positive test, as it resists varus (outward) forces. Similarly, the medial collateral ligament (MCL) is unlikely to cause a positive test, as it resists valgus forces and is not directly involved in the pivot shift mechanism.\n\nStep 4: Consider the remaining options\nThis leaves us with the ACL, PCL, medial meniscus, and lateral meniscus as possible causes of a positive pivot shift test. Of these, the ACL is the most commonly injured structure in the knee, and a positive pivot shift test is often indicative of an ACL tear. The PCL can also cause a positive test, but this is less common. The menisci can also be injured in a way that causes a positive pivot shift test, but this is usually accompanied by other signs and symptoms, such as joint line tenderness and clicking or locking of the knee.\n\nStep 5: Choose the correct answer\nBased on the above analysis, the correct answer is B) Anterior cruciate ligament.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "4ac79e7a-b0e3-4341-bfad-a4a89ba08ea2",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "B) Anterior cruciate ligament.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "B",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
