                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what antigen-presenting cells are?\n\nProfessor: Antigen-presenting cells are cells that display fragments of foreign substances, called antigens, on their surface to activate the immune system.\n\nStudent: What are M-cells?\n\nProfessor: M-cells are specialized cells found in the mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue that transport antigens from the lumen of the gut to the underlying lymphoid tissue.\n\nStudent: What about macrophages?\n\nProfessor: Macrophages are a type of white blood cell that engulf and digest foreign substances, including pathogens and dead cells, and present their antigens to other immune cells.\n\nStudent: And Langerhans cells?\n\nProfessor: Langerhans cells are a type of dendritic cell found in the skin and mucosa that capture and present antigens to other immune cells.\n\nStudent: So, which of these cells is not an antigen-presenting cell?\n\nProfessor: Thymocytes are not antigen-presenting cells. They are immature T cells that develop in the thymus gland and require antigen presentation by other cells to become activated.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "b6139971-0de9-4c23-a0d9-1e1323950bf9",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "D) Thymocytes.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "D",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
