                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Understand the question\nThe question is asking us to identify which cells are not antigen-presenting cells.\n\nStep 2: Know the definition of antigen-presenting cells\nAntigen-presenting cells are cells that display antigens on their surface to activate T cells.\n\nStep 3: Identify the options\nThe options are:\nA) M-cells\nB) Macrophages\nC) Langerhans cells\nD) Thymocytes\n\nStep 4: Eliminate the options\nA) M-cells: M-cells are antigen-presenting cells found in the mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue. Therefore, they are antigen-presenting cells.\nB) Macrophages: Macrophages are antigen-presenting cells that engulf and digest foreign substances. Therefore, they are antigen-presenting cells.\nC) Langerhans cells: Langerhans cells are antigen-presenting cells found in the skin and mucosa. Therefore, they are antigen-presenting cells.\nD) Thymocytes: Thymocytes are immature T cells that develop in the thymus. They do not present antigens to activate T cells. Therefore, they are not antigen-presenting cells.\n\nStep 5: Select the correct answer\nThe correct answer is D) Thymocytes.",
