                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Understand the question\nThe question is asking which vessel is not ligated (tied off) in order to control epistaxis (nosebleed).\n\nStep 2: Review the options\nA) Maxillary artery - This is a branch of the external carotid artery and is sometimes ligated to control severe nosebleeds.\nB) Anterior ethmoidal artery - This is a branch of the ophthalmic artery and is sometimes ligated to control nosebleeds.\nC) Internal carotid artery - This is a major artery that supplies blood to the brain and is not typically ligated for nosebleed control.\nD) External carotid artery - This is a major artery that supplies blood to the face and is sometimes ligated to control severe nosebleeds.\n\nStep 3: Determine the answer\nBased on the options, the answer is C) Internal carotid artery. This artery is not typically ligated for nosebleed control because it is a major artery that supplies blood to the brain and ligation could cause serious complications.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C) Internal carotid artery.",
