                    {
                        "id": "91f1614b-3f7a-4664-9284-2dd2eeb50e97",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-01",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nEpistaxis is a medical condition characterized by bleeding from the nose. In some cases, it may require ligation of blood vessels to control the bleeding. The question asks which vessel is not ligated in case of epistaxis control. \n\nA) Maxillary artery: The maxillary artery is a branch of the external carotid artery and supplies blood to the deep structures of the face. It is not typically ligated in case of epistaxis control.\n\nB) Anterior ethmoidal artery: The anterior ethmoidal artery is a branch of the ophthalmic artery and supplies blood to the nasal cavity. It may be ligated in case of anterior epistaxis (bleeding from the front of the nose).\n\nC) Internal carotid artery: The internal carotid artery is a major blood vessel that supplies blood to the brain. It is not typically ligated in case of epistaxis control.\n\nD) External carotid artery: The external carotid artery is a major blood vessel that supplies blood to the face, scalp, and neck. It may be ligated in case of posterior epistaxis (bleeding from the back of the nose).\n\nTherefore, the vessel that is not ligated in case of epistaxis control is the internal carotid artery (option C).",
                        "answers": [
