                        "cot": "Rephrased question: Which vessel is not tied off during epistaxis control?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n\n- Epistaxis is the medical term for nosebleed.\n- To control a nosebleed, one method is to ligate (tie off) the blood vessels that are bleeding.\n- The options given are all arteries that supply blood to the nose.\n- The vessel that is not ligated during epistaxis control is the one that does not supply blood to the area of the nose that is bleeding.\n\nAnswer: C) Internal carotid artery. \n\nExplanation: The internal carotid artery does not supply blood to the nose, so it is not ligated during epistaxis control. The other options (maxillary artery, anterior ethmoidal artery, and external carotid artery) all supply blood to the nose and may be ligated to control bleeding.",
