                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Epistaxis is a medical condition characterized by bleeding from the nose. It can be caused by various factors such as trauma, inflammation, or underlying medical conditions. In severe cases, epistaxis may require medical intervention to control the bleeding.\n\nTo control epistaxis, ligation of blood vessels may be necessary. Ligation is a surgical procedure that involves tying off a blood vessel to stop bleeding. However, not all blood vessels are ligated in case of epistaxis control.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the purpose of ligating blood vessels in case of epistaxis control?\n2. What are the common blood vessels ligated in case of epistaxis control?\n3. What are the functions of the blood vessels listed in the options?\n\nAnswering sub-question 1: \nThe purpose of ligating blood vessels in case of epistaxis control is to stop the bleeding by blocking the blood flow to the affected area.\n\nAnswering sub-question 2:\nThe common blood vessels ligated in case of epistaxis control are the sphenopalatine artery, anterior ethmoidal artery, and internal maxillary artery.\n\nAnswering sub-question 3:\nA) Maxillary artery - supplies blood to the deep structures of the face, including the maxillary sinus, palate, and upper teeth.\nB) Anterior ethmoidal artery - supplies blood to the nasal septum and lateral wall of the nose.\nC) Internal carotid artery - supplies blood to the brain and eyes.\nD) External carotid artery - supplies blood to the face, scalp, and neck muscles.\n\nAnswering the original question:\nBased on the sub-questions, the blood vessel that is not ligated in case of epistaxis control is the internal carotid artery (option C). Ligation of the internal carotid artery can lead to severe complications such as stroke or blindness. Therefore, it is not a recommended procedure for epistaxis control.",
