                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Understand the question\nThe question is asking which vessel is not ligated in case of epistaxis control. Epistaxis is the medical term for nosebleed. Ligation is a surgical procedure in which a vessel is tied off to prevent bleeding.\n\nStep 2: Identify the vessels\nThe question lists four vessels:\n- Maxillary artery: a branch of the external carotid artery that supplies blood to the face and maxillary sinus\n- Anterior ethmoidal artery: a branch of the ophthalmic artery that supplies blood to the nasal cavity and ethmoid sinus\n- Internal carotid artery: a major artery that supplies blood to the brain and eyes\n- External carotid artery: a major artery that supplies blood to the face, scalp, and neck\n\nStep 3: Eliminate options\nWe are looking for the vessel that is not ligated in case of epistaxis control. Epistaxis control typically involves identifying the bleeding vessel and applying pressure or cauterizing it. Ligation is a more invasive procedure that is usually reserved for severe or recurrent nosebleeds. Therefore, we can eliminate options A, B, and C, as all of these vessels may be ligated in certain cases of epistaxis control.\n\nStep 4: Determine the answer\nThe only option remaining is D, external carotid artery. This artery is not typically ligated in case of epistaxis control, as it supplies blood to many important structures in the head and neck. Ligation of the external carotid artery may result in serious complications, such as stroke or tissue death.\n\nAnswer: D) External carotid artery.",
