                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe best study design for comparing the fat content in the expressed breast milk of pre-term infants to term infants would be a longitudinal study. \n\nHere's why: \n\nA) Case control: This study design is used to compare individuals with a particular condition (cases) to those without the condition (controls). It is not well-suited for comparing the fat content in breast milk between pre-term and term infants because it does not involve following individuals over time. \n\nB) Prospective cohort: This study design involves following a group of individuals over time to see if they develop a particular condition or outcome. While this design could be used to compare the fat content in breast milk between pre-term and term infants, it may not be the best choice because it can be difficult to recruit and retain participants over a long period of time. \n\nC) Longitudinal study: This study design involves following a group of individuals over time and collecting data at multiple time points. This design is well-suited for comparing the fat content in breast milk between pre-term and term infants because it allows for the collection of data at multiple time points, which can help to identify changes in fat content over time. \n\nD) Ambispective: This is not a commonly used study design and is not well-suited for comparing the fat content in breast milk between pre-term and term infants. \n\nTherefore, the best study design for comparing the fat content in the expressed breast milk of pre-term infants to term infants is a longitudinal study.",
