                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep-by-step approach:\n\n1. Start by understanding the basics of genetics and how eye color is inherited. Eye color is determined by multiple genes, but the most important one is called OCA2. This gene comes in two versions: a brown version (B) and a blue version (b). The brown version is dominant, meaning that if a person has at least one copy of the brown version, they will have brown eyes. The blue version is recessive, meaning that a person needs two copies of the blue version to have blue eyes.\n\n2. Look at the eye colors of the baby's parents. If both parents have brown eyes, it is likely that the baby will also have brown eyes, since they both have at least one copy of the brown version of the OCA2 gene. However, if one or both parents have blue eyes, the baby could have blue eyes if they inherit two copies of the blue version of the OCA2 gene (one from each parent).\n\n3. Consider the eye colors of the baby's grandparents and other relatives. This can give you a better idea of the likelihood of the baby having a certain eye color, especially if there is a history of blue or brown eyes in the family.\n\n4. Keep in mind that eye color can sometimes be unpredictable, especially if there is a mix of different genes involved. For example, if one parent has brown eyes but carries a copy of the blue version of the OCA2 gene, and the other parent has blue eyes but carries a copy of the brown version of the OCA2 gene, the baby could end up with any combination of eye colors.\n\n5. Avoid taking a random guess, as this is unlikely to be accurate. Instead, use the information available about the baby's family history and genetics to make an educated guess about their eye color.",
