                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": " \n\nThe cornea is the outermost layer of the eye. It is a clear, curved surface that helps to focus light as it enters the eye. \n\nThe next layer is the aqueous humor, which is a clear, watery fluid that fills the space between the cornea and the iris (the colored part of the eye). \n\nThe iris is the colored part of the eye that helps to control the amount of light that enters the eye. \n\nThe pupil is the black part of the eye that allows light to enter. \n\nThe lens is a clear, curved structure that helps to focus light on the retina (the back of the eye). \n\nThe retina is a thin layer of tissue that lines the back of the eye. It is made up of light-sensitive cells called rods and cones. These cells change light into electrical signals. These signals travel through the optic nerve to the brain. The brain then interprets these signals as images.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "12971cdb-29c8-4023-8be0-b9bc48ae0de9",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
