                        "cot": "How do we sense visual things?\n\nA) nerves\nB) tibia\nC) nostril\nD) cornea\n\nTo sense visual things, we need to use a specific part of our body. Let's go through each option to see which one is used for this purpose.\n\nA) Nerves - While nerves play a crucial role in transmitting signals from our eyes to our brain, they are not the part of our body that senses visual things.\n\nB) Tibia - The tibia is a bone in our leg and has no role in sensing visual things.\n\nC) Nostril - The nostril is used for smelling and has no role in sensing visual things.\n\nD) Cornea - The cornea is a part of our eye that helps to focus light onto the retina, which then sends signals to our brain to create the images we see. Therefore, the correct answer is D) cornea.\n\nAnswer: D) cornea is used for sensing visual things.",
