                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Seals are aquatic mammals that spend most of their time in the water but come ashore to breed, molt, and rest. They are found in both the northern and southern hemispheres and are adapted to a wide range of environments.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What are the natural habitats of seals?\n2. What are the temperature preferences of seals?\n3. What are the geographical ranges of different seal species?\n\nAnswering the sub-questions:\n1. Seals are found in a variety of aquatic habitats, including oceans, seas, lakes, and rivers. They prefer areas with abundant food sources, such as fish, squid, and crustaceans.\n2. Seals have different temperature preferences depending on the species. Some, like the harp seal, are adapted to cold Arctic waters, while others, like the Mediterranean monk seal, prefer warmer waters.\n3. Different seal species have different geographical ranges. For example, the harbor seal is found along the coasts of the North Atlantic and North Pacific oceans, while the leopard seal is found in the Antarctic and sub-Antarctic regions.\n\nAnswer: Based on the sub-questions, it can be concluded that seals are most likely to be found in arctic environments (B). While seals can be found in a variety of aquatic habitats, many species are adapted to cold waters and are found in the Arctic and sub-Arctic regions.",
