                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Decaying vegetation is a natural process that occurs when organic matter breaks down and decomposes over time. This process releases nutrients back into the soil and atmosphere, which can be used by other living organisms.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the process of decaying vegetation?\n2. How does decaying vegetation release nutrients?\n3. How can these nutrients be used by other living organisms?\n4. How do nuclear power, light beams, gas powered motors, and windmills function?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The process of decaying vegetation occurs when organic matter, such as leaves, branches, and other plant material, breaks down and decomposes over time. This process is facilitated by microorganisms, such as bacteria and fungi, which break down the organic matter into simpler compounds.\n2. Decaying vegetation releases nutrients back into the soil and atmosphere. As the organic matter breaks down, it releases carbon dioxide, water, and other compounds that can be used by other living organisms.\n3. These nutrients can be used by other plants to grow and thrive. For example, nitrogen is an essential nutrient for plant growth, and is often released during the process of decaying vegetation.\n4. Nuclear power relies on the process of nuclear fission to generate heat, which is then used to produce electricity. Light beams are produced by the emission of photons from excited atoms or molecules. Gas powered motors use the combustion of fossil fuels to generate mechanical energy. Windmills use the kinetic energy of wind to generate electricity. None of these processes directly rely on decaying vegetation. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is none of the above (E).",
