                        "cot": "Rephrased question: What is the purpose of cellular respiration in cells?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n\n- Cellular respiration is a metabolic process that occurs in cells.\n- It involves the breakdown of glucose (or other organic molecules) to produce ATP, the main energy currency of cells.\n- Therefore, the purpose of cellular respiration is to release energy that cells can use for various functions, such as movement, growth, and maintenance.\n- This energy is obtained by oxidizing glucose (or other organic molecules) through a series of enzymatic reactions that involve electron transport and oxidative phosphorylation.\n- Photosynthesis, on the other hand, is a process that occurs in some cells (e.g., plant cells) to produce glucose (or other organic molecules) using light energy from the sun.\n- Release of waste and meiosis are not directly related to cellular respiration.\n\nAnswer: D) release energy",
