                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, can you please repeat the question?\n\nProfessor: Of course. What causes seasons on Earth?\n\nStudent: Oh, I see. So, what are the options again?\n\nProfessor: The options are A) Our Planet, B) The Atmosphere, C) The Equator, and D) The Sun.\n\nStudent: Okay, I think I know the answer. Is it D) The Sun?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's correct! The Sun is the reason why we have seasons on Earth. Do you want me to explain how it works?\n\nStudent: Yes, please. I'm curious to know.\n\nProfessor: Alright. So, the Earth rotates on its axis, which is an imaginary line that runs through the North and South Poles. As it rotates, different parts of the Earth receive different amounts of sunlight. When the Northern Hemisphere is tilted towards the Sun, it receives more direct sunlight and experiences summer. When it's tilted away from the Sun, it receives less direct sunlight and experiences winter. The opposite is true for the Southern Hemisphere.\n\nStudent: I think I understand now. So, the tilt of the Earth's axis is what causes the seasons?\n\nProfessor: Exactly! The tilt of the Earth's axis is what causes the different parts of the Earth to receive different amounts of sunlight, which in turn causes the seasons.",
