                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain how to combine two substances together?\n\nProfessor: Sure, what substances are you trying to combine?\n\nStudent: I don't know, can you give me an example?\n\nProfessor: Sure, let's say we want to combine water and oil.\n\nStudent: Okay, how do we do that?\n\nProfessor: Well, we know that oil and water don't mix, so we need to add something to help them mix.\n\nStudent: What can we add?\n\nProfessor: We can add a substance called an emulsifier, which helps to mix oil and water together.\n\nStudent: How do we add the emulsifier?\n\nProfessor: We can add it to the water first, and then slowly add the oil while stirring constantly.\n\nStudent: How much emulsifier do we need?\n\nProfessor: It depends on the amount of water and oil you are trying to mix. Generally, you want to use about 1-2% emulsifier by weight of the total mixture.\n\nStudent: Okay, so once we add the emulsifier and mix the oil and water together, will they stay mixed?\n\nProfessor: Yes, they should stay mixed as long as the emulsifier is present. If the mixture sits for a long time, the oil and water may eventually separate again.\n\nStudent: Got it, thanks for explaining that to me.",
