                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, can you explain how medicine can cause allergic reactions?\n\nProfessor: Sure, when we take medicine, it enters our body and interacts with our immune system. Sometimes, our immune system can mistake the medicine as a harmful substance and trigger an allergic reaction.\n\nStudent: What kind of allergic reactions can medicine cause?\n\nProfessor: Medicine can cause a range of allergic reactions, from mild to severe. Some common ones include skin rashes, hives, itching, and swelling. More severe reactions can include difficulty breathing, anaphylaxis, and even death.\n\nStudent: Can you give an example of a medicine that can cause an allergic reaction?\n\nProfessor: Yes, penicillin is a common medication that can cause allergic reactions in some people. It's important to note that not everyone who takes penicillin will have an allergic reaction, but those who do can experience symptoms ranging from mild to severe.\n\nStudent: How can someone know if they are allergic to a medicine?\n\nProfessor: If someone suspects they may be allergic to a medicine, they should speak with their doctor. The doctor may perform an allergy test to determine if the person is allergic to the medication. It's important to note that if someone has had an allergic reaction to a medication in the past, they should avoid taking it again in the future.\n\nStudent: Thank you for explaining that, Professor. It's important to know about the potential risks of taking medicine.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "aec67292-4f69-4ff7-9ff4-65190e53d0fa",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
