                        "cot_trigger": "zhou-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: What is phloem?\nPhloem is a type of plant tissue that transports organic compounds, such as sugars and amino acids, from the leaves to other parts of the plant.\n\nStep 2: How does phloem move things around a plant?\nPhloem uses a process called translocation, which involves the movement of organic compounds from a source (usually the leaves) to a sink (usually the roots or other growing parts of the plant). This movement is facilitated by specialized cells called sieve tubes, which are connected end-to-end to form a continuous pathway for the flow of materials.\n\nStep 3: Which of the options given is the best analogy for how phloem moves things around a plant?\nA) blood moves in a body - This is the best analogy, as both blood and phloem transport materials around a system (the body or the plant, respectively).\nB) leaves move in the wind - This is not a good analogy, as leaves are not actively transporting anything, but rather being moved by external forces.\nC) water moves in a system - This is a partial analogy, as water can also be transported by phloem, but it does not capture the specific function of phloem in moving organic compounds.\nD) cars move on a street - This is not a good analogy, as cars are not a natural part of a plant's physiology and do not perform a similar function to phloem. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A) blood moves in a body.",
                        "answers": [
