                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Phloem is a type of plant tissue that transports sugars and other nutrients from the leaves to other parts of the plant.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the function of phloem in a plant?\n2. How does phloem transport nutrients?\n3. What are some similarities between phloem and the options given in the question?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The function of phloem in a plant is to transport sugars and other nutrients from the leaves to other parts of the plant, such as the roots, stems, and flowers.\n2. Phloem transports nutrients through a process called translocation, which involves the movement of sugars and other molecules from areas of high concentration to areas of low concentration. This process is facilitated by specialized cells called sieve tubes and companion cells.\n3. Option A, blood moves in a body, is the most similar to how phloem moves things around a plant. Both systems involve the transportation of nutrients and other substances throughout the organism. Option B, leaves move in the wind, and option D, cars move on a street, are not similar to phloem at all. Option C, water moves in a system, is somewhat similar to phloem in that both involve the movement of fluids, but water does not transport nutrients in the same way that phloem does.",
