                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The correct answer is D) An insulated room.\n\nIn January in Alaska, the temperature can drop to extremely low levels, which can put your dog at risk of hypothermia. Hypothermia occurs when the body loses heat faster than it can produce it, leading to a dangerously low body temperature.\n\nTo ensure that your dog remains free from hypothermia in January in Alaska, you need to provide it with a warm and insulated environment. This can be achieved by providing your dog with an insulated room that is free from drafts and has adequate heating. This will help to keep your dog warm and prevent it from losing body heat.\n\nWhile lots of meat and vitamin supplements can be beneficial for your dog's overall health, they will not necessarily prevent hypothermia. Similarly, while love and affection are important for your dog's emotional well-being, they will not provide the physical warmth and protection that your dog needs to stay safe in cold weather.",
