                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The correct answer is C) a lit candle in a window signalling to someone.\n\nTo explain why, we need to understand that fire is a source of light. When something burns, it releases energy in the form of heat and light. So, any time we see a flame, we know that it is giving off light.\n\nOption A talks about an oven being preheated and the pilot light being lit. While this does involve fire, it doesn't necessarily give off visible light. The pilot light is usually a small flame that is used to ignite the main burner, but it may not be visible to the naked eye.\n\nOption B talks about a match being lit to light a cigarette. This is a good example of fire giving off light, but it is not the best answer to the question. The light from the match is usually very brief and not very bright, so it may not be as noticeable as other sources of light.\n\nOption D talks about a fire that was put out to send smoke signals. While this does involve fire, it doesn't give off visible light. Instead, it produces smoke that can be seen from a distance.\n\nOption C, on the other hand, talks about a lit candle in a window signalling to someone. This is a great example of fire giving off light. A candle flame is usually very visible and can be seen from a distance, especially if it is placed in a window. It is also a common way to signal to someone that you are home or need help. Therefore, option C is the correct answer to the question.",
