                    {
                        "id": "690981c5-8c05-4284-aa71-e941e28225d4",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-13",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "How can fire give off light? Which of the following is an example of this phenomenon?\n\nA) When an oven is preheated and the pilot light is lit, it is an example of fire giving off light. The pilot light is a small flame that burns continuously to ignite the main burner when needed.\n\nB) Lighting a match to light a cigarette is another example of fire giving off light. The heat generated by the friction of striking the matchstick ignites the chemicals on the match head, producing a flame that emits light.\n\nC) A lit candle in a window signaling to someone is also an example of fire giving off light. The heat of the flame melts the wax, which is drawn up the wick by capillary action and vaporized by the heat. The vaporized wax then reacts with oxygen in the air to produce a flame that emits light.\n\nD) A fire that was put out to send smoke signals is not an example of fire giving off light. Smoke signals are created by burning materials that produce thick smoke, not by producing light.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A, B, and C.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "2e20c998-84db-4057-b713-f32e328a087c",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "A, B, and C.",
                                "answer_from_choices": null,
                                "correct_answer": null
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
                        "date": "2023/03/30 03:00:27",
