                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Electromagnets require an electric current to create a magnetic field. The strength of the magnetic field can be increased by increasing the current or by increasing the number of wire coils in the electromagnet.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the current power source being used?\n2. Is the wire being used the appropriate gauge for the desired current?\n3. Are the wire coils tightly wound and evenly spaced?\n4. Is there any resistance in the circuit that could be causing a drop in voltage?\n5. Is the power source capable of providing enough current to create the desired magnetic field strength?\n\nAnswer:\n1. The current power source being used should be checked to ensure it is providing enough voltage and current to power the electromagnet.\n2. The wire gauge being used should be appropriate for the desired current. Thicker wire can handle more current without overheating.\n3. The wire coils should be tightly wound and evenly spaced to ensure a consistent magnetic field.\n4. Any resistance in the circuit, such as a loose connection or a damaged wire, should be identified and fixed to prevent a drop in voltage.\n5. The power source should be capable of providing enough current to create the desired magnetic field strength. If not, a stronger power source may be needed.",
                        "answers": [
