                        "cot": "Echolocation is a method used by animals like bats and dolphins to navigate and find objects by emitting sounds and listening to the echoes that return. These echoes can provide information about the distance, shape, and size of an object.\n\nA) Distance - Echolocation can detect the distance of an object based on the time it takes for the emitted sound to return as an echo.\nB) Shape - Echolocation can detect the shape of an object as the sound waves bounce off the object's surface, creating a unique echo pattern.\nC) Size - Echolocation can detect the size of an object as larger objects will produce stronger echoes, while smaller objects will produce weaker echoes.\n\nD) Temperature - Echolocation does not provide information about an object's temperature, as it relies on sound waves rather than heat or thermal energy.\n\nSo, the correct answer is:\n\nD) temperature",
