                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-12",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Echolocation is a biological sonar system used by some animals, such as bats and dolphins, to navigate and locate prey. It works by emitting high-frequency sound waves and then listening for the echoes that bounce back off of objects in the environment. Based on the timing and characteristics of these echoes, the animal can determine the location and some properties of the objects.\n\nThe question is asking which property of an object echolocation cannot detect. Let's consider each option:\n\nA) Distance: Echolocation is specifically designed to detect distance. By measuring the time it takes for the sound waves to bounce back, the animal can calculate the distance to the object.\n\nB) Shape: Echolocation can provide some information about the shape of an object. For example, if the sound waves bounce back from different parts of the object at different times, the animal can infer that the object has a certain shape.\n\nC) Size: Echolocation can also provide some information about the size of an object. If the sound waves bounce back with a certain intensity, the animal can infer that the object is larger or smaller.\n\nD) Temperature: Echolocation is not designed to detect temperature. It relies on sound waves, which are not affected by temperature in the same way that light or heat waves are.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D) temperature.",
