                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The lunar cycle is approximately 29.5 days long, which means that there is one full moon every 29.5 days. Since there are 12 months in a year, we can calculate the number of full moons in a year by dividing 12 by the length of the lunar cycle in months:\n\n12 months / 29.5 days per lunar cycle = 12/29.5 full moons per month\n\nRounding to the nearest whole number, we get:\n\n12/29.5 \u2248 0.41\n\nSo, on average, there are about 0.41 full moons per month. Since we can't have a fraction of a full moon, we need to round up or down to the nearest whole number. If we round down, we get 0 full moons per month, which is obviously incorrect. If we round up, we get 1 full moon per month, which is the correct answer.\n\nTherefore, the answer is C) once.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "e3700546-5619-4306-a8e9-7d9adf1cb367",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
