                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step-by-step procedure:\n1. Identify the problem: ice buildup on a sidewalk.\n2. Determine the goal: reduce the ice.\n3. Consider the options: adding salt, adding litter, adding sand, adding water.\n4. Evaluate each option based on effectiveness, cost, and potential negative consequences.\n5. Choose the best option based on the evaluation.\n\nGoing through each step:\n1. The problem is clearly stated as ice buildup on a sidewalk.\n2. The goal is to reduce the ice.\n3. The options are: adding salt, adding litter, adding sand, adding water.\n4. Evaluating each option:\n- Adding salt: Salt lowers the freezing point of water, which can melt the ice. However, it can be expensive and can damage plants and concrete. It can also harm pets and wildlife if ingested.\n- Adding litter: Litter can provide traction and absorb moisture, but it does not melt the ice and can be unsightly and difficult to clean up.\n- Adding sand: Sand provides traction and can help melt the ice by absorbing sunlight and generating heat. It is relatively inexpensive and does not harm the environment. However, it can be messy and difficult to clean up.\n- Adding water: Adding water can create a thin layer of ice that is easier to remove, but it can also make the problem worse if the water freezes again. It is not an effective solution.\n\n5. Based on the evaluation, the best option is adding sand. It is effective, relatively inexpensive, and does not harm the environment.",
