                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Ice buildup on a sidewalk can be dangerous and cause slips and falls. \n\nSub-questions:\n1. How does salt reduce ice buildup?\n2. How does litter reduce ice buildup?\n3. How does sand reduce ice buildup?\n4. How does water reduce ice buildup?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Salt reduces ice buildup by lowering the freezing point of water. When salt is added to ice, it dissolves and creates a brine solution that has a lower freezing point than water. This causes the ice to melt and prevents new ice from forming.\n2. Litter does not reduce ice buildup. In fact, it can make the situation worse by creating a slippery surface and obstructing drainage systems.\n3. Sand reduces ice buildup by providing traction on the surface. When sand is spread on ice, it creates a rough surface that helps prevent slips and falls. It does not melt the ice, but it can make it easier to walk on.\n4. Water does not reduce ice buildup. In fact, it can make the situation worse by creating a layer of ice on top of the existing ice.",
                        "answers": [
