                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, can you explain how we can reduce ice buildup on a sidewalk?\n\nProfessor: Sure, there are different ways to reduce ice buildup. Which option do you think is the correct one?\n\nStudent: I'm not sure. Can you give me some options?\n\nProfessor: Of course. The options are A) adding salt, B) adding litter, C) adding sand, and D) adding water.\n\nStudent: Hmm, I think it might be adding salt. Is that correct?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's correct. Adding salt lowers the freezing point of water, which makes it harder for ice to form.\n\nStudent: That makes sense. But how much salt should we add?\n\nProfessor: Good question. The amount of salt depends on the temperature and the amount of ice. As a general rule, you should use about 2-3 pounds of salt per 1000 square feet of sidewalk.\n\nStudent: Okay, I understand. But what if we don't have salt? Can we use litter or sand instead?\n\nProfessor: Yes, you can. Litter and sand don't melt the ice, but they provide traction and make it easier to walk on the sidewalk. However, they are not as effective as salt in reducing ice buildup.\n\nStudent: I see. And what about adding water? Would that work?\n\nProfessor: No, adding water would actually make the ice worse. Water would freeze and create more ice on the sidewalk. So, it's important to avoid adding water in this situation.\n\nStudent: Got it. Thank you for explaining that to me, Professor.\n\nProfessor: You're welcome. Always happy to help.",
